    title: "Old circuit boards are scanned into the company's VR game",
    summary: "Fazbear Entertainment has a virtual-reality game made out of its own history, and sends the studio old circuit boards to scan into it. The company admits that some stories about it \"were loosely based on actual events\" [[hw-intro-loosely-based-on-actual-events]], calls the majority of them fabrications \"from the mind of a complete lunatic\" [[hw-intro-complete-lunatic]], and offers the game as a way to laugh them off [[hw-intro-vr-game-to-move-past-ghost-stories]]. Code on the boards \"seemed to take hold by itself\", and then \"he started appearing\" [[hw-tape-05]]: a grinning, person-shaped figure in a yellow rabbit costume [[hw-rabbit-figure-appearance]]. The tapes call it \"the anomaly\" [[hw-tape-girl-calls-it-the-anomaly]]; it is the rabbit in the code (known as Glitchtrap, Scott's working name for it). Which machine the boards came from, no game says. The first tester, Jeremy, has nightmares and stops jumping at scares: \"He just stands there like he's talking to someone.\" [[hw-tape-07]] The tapes imply that he cut his own face, and never say that he died [[hw-jeremy-self-injury-implied]]. The second tester hides tapes in the game with instructions for whoever comes next [[hw-tape-16]]. One tape says that Fazbear Entertainment itself \"hired the game developer\" behind earlier games about it, games \"designed to conceal and make light of what happened\" [[hw-tape-13-fe-hired-developer]].",
